      "summary": "This Cochrane review examined beginning-reading instruction for children and adolescents with intellectual disability. Seven controlled studies involving 352 participants met its criteria, and all delivered teaching in school settings. The review found benefits for phonological awareness, word reading and decoding. Certainty varied by outcome, and the fluency estimate was imprecise. Important concerns included the difficulty of blinding teachers and participants and limited assessor blinding. The findings support including learners with intellectual disability in explicit literacy teaching rather than assuming that only picture matching is appropriate. Adults should identify the actual spellings and blending steps a learner needs, provide enough modelling and revisit them in useful contexts. These findings concern teaching interventions, not the effectiveness of this app. Results differ by measured skill.",
