      "summary": "This synthesis reviewed reading-comprehension interventions for autistic pupils in kindergarten through secondary school. Twelve studies met its criteria, most using single-case designs. Instruction included generating questions, graphic organisers, predictions, explicit teaching, reference-word cues and different grouping arrangements. The authors suggested that adaptations of comprehension teaching used with other struggling readers may also help autistic students. Most studies measured comprehension with researcher-created tasks, and few reported standardised outcomes. That matters because doing well on a familiar activity may not show the same benefit on new reading material. Parents and teachers can explicitly teach a chosen comprehension process and discuss meaning, while watching for use beyond the taught examples. The review does not establish a universally effective approach or validate an app-based course.",
