      "summary": "This review considered reading instruction for autistic learners across phonemic awareness, phonics, fluency, vocabulary and comprehension. It identified 11 studies involving 61 participants aged 4–17. The authors concluded that autistic children can benefit from instruction consistent with reading research, while emphasising that the evidence base was preliminary. Its useful educational message is inclusion in explicit literacy instruction, not an assumption that autism requires an entirely separate set of reading principles. Individual children can nevertheless need different communication, sensory or instructional supports. The small evidence base cannot establish which activity works best for a particular learner or predict progress from game completion. Adults should identify the component skill being practised and check whether learning appears with new material as well as the familiar activity.",
