      "summary": "This trial evaluated a manualised social-communication intervention for children with pragmatic communication needs, with or without autism-related features. Eighty-eight children were allocated to the programme or usual treatment. The programme involved up to 20 specialist-delivered sessions. Benefits appeared on ratings of conversational competence, pragmatic functioning and classroom learning, but not on the primary standardised structural-language outcome or narrative measure. This is a useful example of why an intervention can help a meaningful communication outcome without improving every language skill. Adults should name the specific goal and measure it in the situations that matter. Cooperative games may create opportunities to communicate through speech, signs or AAC, but those opportunities are not the intervention tested here. The findings should not be generalised to every autistic child or every communication app.",
