      "summary": "This study explored a parent-supported shared-book-reading programme for Italian-speaking preschoolers with developmental language disorder. Thirty-two children participated; twenty families used the programme and twelve formed a comparison group. All children were also receiving speech and language therapy. After eight weeks, participating mothers used some taught strategies more often, and children showed greater engagement and more verbal production during shared reading. Several other outcomes, including language complexity and diversity, did not improve. Because allocation was not random and concurrent therapy continued, the findings require cautious interpretation. The study suggests that enjoyable shared interaction can be a useful context for language support. It does not establish that viewing pictures alone, app-only reading or every parent strategy produces general language gains, and it should not be applied automatically across languages.",
